WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.160 --> 00:00:02.880
I’d rather have a science balloon

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:05.140
than a science satellite

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:07.700
With a balloon, there’s no need for rockets.

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:11.120
To get a satellite to hover over the same spot on earth

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:13.200
-- geostationary orbit-- you

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:16.360
you have to be 36 thousand km away --

00:00:16.360 --> 00:00:19.700
too far to use some sensors, like radar

00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:22.660
A balloon can sit a 1000 times closer --

00:00:22.660 --> 00:00:26.840
in the stratosphere, 30 to 40 km above the earth,

00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:29.880
and still be above 99% of the atmosphere,

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:31.480
with clear views of the stars,

00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:33.900
or wide panoramas of Earth.

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:35.600
You can bring down a balloon,

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:38.000
tinker with on-board scientific equipment--

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:39.440
and then launch again.

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:41.880
Satellites, not so much.

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:43.880
Cost is also a factor.

00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:46.780
NOAA’s four new geostationary weather satellites

00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:49.880
are estimated to cost $11 billion.

00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:51.240
Whereas a balloon flight

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:55.160
might cost just a few hundred thousand dollars.

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:57.880
NASA has been flying balloons for a long time,

00:00:57.880 --> 00:01:00.240
but without any steering capabilities,

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:03.420
they drift in the wind across continents.

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:05.020
Now private industry

00:01:05.020 --> 00:01:07.220
is getting in the balloon game,

00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:09.780
with new technologies that are enabling balloons

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:12.080
to hover over one spot.

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:15.520
These balloons rise or fall in the stratosphere,

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:17.740
catching countervailing breezes that allow

00:01:17.740 --> 00:01:20.940
them to stay roughly in the same place.

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:22.140
What can a balloon

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:23.960
with a scientific payload do?

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:26.500
It can look down at the Earth,

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:30.320
checking on storms or animal migrations.

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:32.060
It can look out into space--

00:01:32.060 --> 00:01:33.680
peering at the surface of the sun

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:36.180
or collecting cosmic rays.

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:38.860
It can even do things in the stratosphere,

00:01:38.860 --> 00:01:41.140
like study how ice crystals could reflect

00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:44.180
sunlight and cool the planet.

00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:46.420
How do you keep a balloon stable enough

00:01:46.420 --> 00:01:48.130
to use a telescope?

00:01:48.130 --> 00:01:50.140
Gyroscopes can help keep a platform

00:01:50.140 --> 00:01:51.740
pointed at its target--

00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:54.660
even as sits in a twisting gondola

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:57.420
But this isn’t an ad for scientific balloons
--

00:01:57.420 --> 00:01:58.860
there are a few caveats

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:01.740
For example, while satellites last for years,

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:05.160
the longest balloon flights have been a few months.

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:07.520
And what goes up needs to come down --

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:09.680
so most balloons come with parachutes

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:13.320
that can bring their payloads down gently.

